---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

// His own column, translated in full and in order. Nothing added, nothing
// dropped, and the dashes the russian leans on are traded for the punctuation
// the rest of this site uses.
const title = "why a product person should stay human, and why that is good for business";
const description =
  "dmytro klochko on motivation, burnout and saying no. vc.ru, translated.";
---

<Layout title={`${title} · dmytro klochko`} description={description} current="home" unlisted>
  <section class="shell session piece" data-scene="fire" aria-labelledby="w2">
    <p class="piece-up">
      <a href="/writing"><span class="arw" aria-hidden="true">↖</span>writing</a>
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <h1 class="card-title" id="w2">{title}</h1>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">on building a team people want to stay in.</p>

    <p class="piece-by">
      dmytro klochko, product manager at skyeng, for vc.ru.{" "}
      <a
        href="https://vc.ru/hr/192417-pochemu-prodaktu-vazhno-ostavatsya-chelovekom-i-pochemu-eto-polezno-dlya-biznesa"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
      >the original, in russian<span class="arw">↗</span></a>
    </p>

    <div class="prose piece-body">
      <p>
        i think kpis are an insufficient tool for motivation, and sometimes an
        unnecessary one that simply doesn't work. real motivation always comes
        from inside, and it matters more than any metric, both for the team and
        for the business. people work at a company because the company has
        expectations and the person has expectations. if they match, people work
        together. when they stop matching, you part ways. and for that inner
        motivation to work, you have to understand what you want, why you're doing
        it, and who you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        on a team there is always the temptation to see people as the functions
        they perform. but people are still people, whether we like it or not. the
        first thing i tried to say, and the thing that helped me and the team, is
        that we all need to remember we're people. with our own thoughts, our own
        tiredness, but people. it's useful to ask yourself now and then: do i
        remember that i'm talking to a human being?
      </p>
      <p>
        i can't imagine standing over someone checking exactly how they work or
        how they reach a result. sometimes a team just needs to be left to work:
        engineers to write code, designers to make meaning. a lot of managers are
        afraid their trust will be abused, so they build an environment where an
        engineer is afraid that without him the whole thing collapses. to stop
        being afraid of that, you just have to come to an agreement. you make
        agreements with people outside of work, so why not inside a company?
      </p>
      <p>
        as a product person, i need to understand why we're doing something. not
        what we're doing for the business, which metric we're moving, but why we
        are on one team at all. my own answer is this: together we're trying to
        make changes that bring value to a user. that doesn't mean everyone on my
        team has to be chasing change. i know brilliant engineers who wanted to
        write code and earn well, and it never once stopped us doing great things.
      </p>
      <p>
        i don't believe in imposing your expectations. if i go to an engineer and
        say, come on, let's strive for change, he'll nod at me and then tell his
        friends at the bar what an idiot i am. my aim is different: i say what i
        want, he says what he wants, and we agree how we'll help each other. i'll
        give him comfort and money, say, and he'll give me change.
      </p>
      <p>
        if it's hard for you to work with someone, it's hard for both of you. in my
        experience, if you want to part with someone, sooner or later you will,
        and until then you'll both suffer. this isn't only, or even mostly, about
        firing. often it's a move to another team where the work will be great and
        interesting for them. yes, you might pay someone compensation, but that
        money comes back many times over: once you stop tolerating the discomfort,
        you can put more attention and energy into the work.
      </p>
      <p>
        working fast matters. it's great to make mistakes fast and move fast,
        because you learn faster that way. but on the other hand, never forget that
        we're working for the long run. the manager's job is to understand that
        life doesn't end on friday with the sprint, and to make sure nobody burns
        out.
      </p>
      <p>
        when someone from outside asks you to pull a task off, it's tempting to
        feed your manager ego and say, "yes, i'll pull it off!" but it's the team
        that does it, and that's bad. my job as a manager is not to be great, it's
        to deliver for a long time in a way the team also enjoys. so it's better
        not to feed the ego: take the hit, say we won't pull it off, let nobody
        praise you, and the team will be fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        at the same time, tasks are like gas, or a cat: they expand to fill all the
        space you have, so the frame around them has to be held hard. if you have
        a lot of tasks, you're always focused on something and can't invent
        anything new, no abstract ideas appear. so i try to give my team a maker's
        day: a day with no meetings and, ideally, no reports.
      </p>
      <p>
        my best ideas, big restructurings among them, almost always came when i
        wasn't overloaded. when i was running the conversation clubs, for example,
        i was busy all the time: growing metrics a little, shipping features,
        closing tasks. then i started doing maker's days, and that was the day i
        began using my own product, just clicking every button. and i realised that
        the best part for me is talking to another person without waiting your turn
        to speak or waiting for the teacher's feedback.
      </p>
      <p>
        that's when i came up with dating clubs: you come in together, but you're
        split into pairs and you rotate. so you speak far more, the teacher still
        moves between pairs, gives feedback and listens. it let us grow the group
        in a single club, lowered the operational cost, and added more of those
        "aha" moments for people.
      </p>
      <p>
        if a task has to get done but the team is tired, find an alternative: cut
        the scope, find an outsourcer, or solve it another way entirely. and then
        defend why that option is better than the overload.
      </p>
      <p>
        saying no is very important. you're not working for two weeks, where you
        could say yes to everything and work through the nights. sometimes all it
        takes is asking why a task that seems urgent and important needs doing at
        all. a manager brings you a task: we're building an integration in two
        days. why are we building it? what will it bring us? the answers to those
        questions change a lot. we decided we only agree to things with
        stakeholders once we've counted the result.
      </p>
      <p>
        we'd recently built an integration with a partner. then a manager comes
        and asks us to rebuild it, and we started estimating. three weeks of work,
        very important to do. so i ask: "why are we doing this? we shipped the
        first version. how many payments came through in two weeks?" turns out,
        two payments. even if we doubled the conversion, the cost of the
        engineering hours is far higher than anything we could earn. so we paused
        until traffic actually started coming from the partner. in the end the
        partnership didn't pay off and the integration was closed entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        communication eats a lot of time and energy. sometimes it's very useful,
        but the less we talk, the better the talking is. we try to solve things in
        writing wherever possible. and if a meeting is needed, it's worth thinking
        about whether the whole team has to be there or whether you can answer for
        them. you can also break a meeting into stages and invite people to just
        one of them, so they aren't sitting around.
      </p>
      <p>
        happy people want to work and stay longer, and they have more expertise.
        and people who also understand why they're doing something do it with more
        energy. everyone wins in the end: the business doesn't have to spend on
        hiring from outside, because its own people are comfortably making a great
        product.
      </p>
      <p>
        everyone works in a sine wave. in their productive stretches people work
        brilliantly and charged up, and then they get tired. i've heard from a lot
        of people that when the energy runs out they start looking for the problem
        inside themselves, looking for new frameworks, new ways to recharge. so
        you need an atmosphere of freedom and care, where it's natural to come in
        and say: "everyone, being tired is normal, let's not die at work."
      </p>
      <p>
        the received wisdom is that you should always be resourced. so everyone
        feels guilty for being tired. and that's the moment when it matters most to
        catch someone, and instead of "go drink some water and get back to work"
        say "ah, that's normal, let's look at your tasks, maybe we cut your load,"
        or even just "don't push it, it'll get better, take your time." to build
        that atmosphere you have to be open yourself and say your own problems out
        loud. and explain why you're asking, so the person doesn't wonder why
        you're suddenly on their case. give the context: "i like working with you,
        i'm interested in how you are and what's going on, i'd like to help."
      </p>
      <p>
        i'm not shy about sending emoji, because you never know when someone is
        burning out, wants to quit, has someone close to them ill, or simply hates
        all of it already, and a little heart really does help a bit. you can go
        further and make personal emoji out of people's photographs.
      </p>
      <p>
        everyone needs to know that they exist, that they're an important part of
        the team. humanity is the ability to keep the focus on your team's values
        all the time, so that everyone on it is well, and it can focus on the work
        and do it brilliantly.
      </p>
      <p>
        i think the main thing in talking to a team is to be brave and not fear
        what people will think of you when you make one decision or another. time
        puts everything in its place: the tickets, the edits and the syncs will be
        forgotten, but you tend to remember yourself and your decisions clearly.
        and if you were real, it was all worth it and you've nothing to be ashamed
        of in front of yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        it was a hard year and everyone is spent. i think what will be missing next
        year is humanity, attentive management. you'll set high goals again and
        you'll hit them, but don't forget why you're doing it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a class="onward onward--back" href="/writing">
      <span class="arw" aria-hidden="true">↖</span><span>the other piece</span>
    </a>
  </section>
</Layout>
